
=== Docstrings

Docstrings are specialized comments that provide extra information about the
program for use during code generation. Here we will focus on how these
docstrings modify CLI generation, but they also inform the generation of rich
APIs (see the HTTP/TCP/socket section).

Docstrings can be attached to type definitions, not just function signatures.
This is useful for avoiding repetition. Without type-level docstrings, you would
need to annotate every use of a type:

[source, morloc]
----
decode ::
  --' The secret key to use for decoding
  --' @metavar KEY
  Key ->
  --' The ciphertext to decode
  --' @metavar CIPHERTEXT
  CipherText ->
  --' The decoded plaintext
  PlainText

encode ::
  --' The secret key to use for encoding
  --' @metavar KEY
  Key ->
  --' The plaintext to encode
  --' @metavar PLAINTEXT
  PlainText ->
  --' The encoded ciphertext
  CipherText
----

The descriptions and metavar names are repeated for every occurrence of `Key`,
`CipherText`, and `PlainText`. Instead, you can attach docstrings directly to
the type definitions:

[source, morloc]
----
--' A secret key
--' @metavar KEY
type Key = Str

--' An encrypted message
--' @metavar CIPHERTEXT
type CipherText = Str

--' A decrypted message
--' @metavar PLAINTEXT
type PlainText = Str

--' Decode a ciphertext with a key
decode :: Key -> CipherText -> PlainText

--' Encode a plaintext with a key
encode :: Key -> PlainText -> CipherText
----

The CLI and API generators will pull the descriptions, tags, and metavar names
from the type definitions automatically. This keeps function signatures clean and
ensures consistent documentation across every function that uses these types.

==== Directive syntax

A docstring line is either *prose* (a plain description) or a *directive*. A
directive begins with `@`:

----
@keyword [arguments...]
----

Every compiler-recognized keyword is written with a leading `@`, and every
argument that refers to a function parameter by position is written with a
leading `$` (`$1` is the first argument, `$2` the second, and so on). Any line
that does not start with `@` is prose, so a description may freely contain a
colon (`Example: ...`) without being mistaken for a directive. To begin a prose
line with a literal `@`, escape it as `\@`.

Docstring directives specify how the arguments of the exported functions map to
CLI positional or optional arguments. Here is a list of the currently supported
directives:

 * `@name` - give the CLI subcommand a dedicated name rather than defaulting to
   the Morloc function name

 * `@unroll` - unroll the record argument into a group of optional arguments

 * `@default` - the default value for an argument, in JSON format. `@default`
   is only meaningful for optional arguments (those declared with `@arg` so
   they can be omitted on the command line).

 * `@metavar` - the variable name used for the argument in the usage text

 * `@arg` - short and long labels for this argument (e.g., `-v/--verbose`)

 * `@true` - the flag labels that toggle a boolean argument on

 * `@false` - the flag labels that toggle a boolean argument off

 * `@many` - for a list-typed positional, accept multiple argv tokens (one per element)

 * `@stdin` - mark a `Str`/path positional as optional and read it from standard
   input when omitted (or given `-`), so the command can act as a Unix filter.
   See "Reading a stream from standard input" below.

 * `@return` - a description of the returned data (this directive is the same as adding a description docstring to the final type in a signature)

 * `@source` / `@form` / `@check.<kind>` and the per-element `@list.source` /
   `@list.form` / `@list.check.<kind>` - control how the CLI accepts data for
   an argument (a path to a file? raw bytes? a packet? newline-separated
   elements?). See the next section.

 * `@with` / `@render` - attach a compile-time output *formatter* to the export
   as a command-scoped flag; invoking the flag routes the output through a named
   function before it hits the wire. Used for stdout formatters, file sinks, and
   reformatters that swap the return type. See "Output actions" below.

`@many`, `@unroll`, and `@stdin` are boolean flags: writing the bare directive
(e.g. `@many`) turns the flag on. Their absence is the default (off).


